# two-envs.config — `M154g` step 4a: the config operand for `C92` (`config:directive:env`) and
# `C94` (`config:directive:session`). Copied into a scratch directory as `tflw.config` by
# `scripts/verify-construct-acceptance.mjs`; see `named-service.tflw` for why the config is the
# file that varies here and the `.tflw` is the file that does not.
#
# Everything in it exists to make **one** thing differ between `env one` and `env two`:
#
# * `api extra` is declared by `one` and not by `two`, so `named-service.tflw` is clean under one
#   and a `TF026` under the other.
# * `session scoped` carries `for env one`; `session everywhere` carries no clause at all. The two
#   are otherwise identical — same login step, same capture, same header — so the `TF028` that
#   `scoped-session.tflw` collects under `--env two` can only be the clause.
#
# Neither session ever runs. The steps inside them are shaped like the root config's `admin`
# session so the file reads as a real config rather than a stub, but every leg that uses this file
# is a `tflw check`, which resolves sessions without executing them.

env one default
  api "http://localhost:4001/v1"
  api extra "http://localhost:4002/v1"

env two
  api "http://localhost:4001/v1"

session scoped for env one
  api POST /auth/login body { email: "c94@example.test", password: "c94" }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.accessToken as token
  header "Authorization" is "Bearer {token}"

session everywhere
  api POST /auth/login body { email: "c94@example.test", password: "c94" }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.accessToken as token
  header "Authorization" is "Bearer {token}"
